hello in this video i will explain  in a simple way what the p-value is i  will start with the question  what is the p-value how do i interpret  the p-value  and what is the difference between the  p-value and  the significance level afterwards i will  show you  how you can calculate p-value easily  online with data tab  for different hypotheses tests in order  to explain the p-value  let's have a look at this example in  this example we want to investigate  if there's a difference in the salary  between women  and men our assumption is that there's  no difference  in the salary so our null hypothesis is  there's no difference between the salary  of man  and woman in the population at the one  side  we have the population but for sure it  is not possible to ask  every woman and man from this population  therefore  we need a sample in this sample we also  have the salary from woman  and man so for sure although in the  population  there's no difference between the salary  in this sample  for sure there will be at least a small  difference  it is quite unlikely that the difference  will be zero  for example in this sample the result  could be  that the difference is 50 euro or  150 euro or 250 euro  even if there's no difference in the  population  but again the assumption is there's no  difference in the salary between men  and women in the population and now  we will come to the p-value the p-value  tells us how likely it is to draw a  sample  in which the salary of men and women  differ  by more than 50 euro or  the p-value tells us how likely it is  to draw a sample in which the salary of  men and women  differ by more than 150 euro  or the last example the p-value tells us  how likely it is to draw a sample in  which the salary  of men and women differ by more than 250  euro  so again we assume that there's no  difference in the population the p-value  now tells us  how likely it is to draw a sample  in which the difference between men and  women in salary  is 250 euro or greater although  there's no difference in the population  for example if we are using data tab to  calculate the p-value  and let's say for the salary difference  of 250 euro  there's a p-value of 0.0  and what does it mean if the p-value  at a difference of 250 euro is for  example  0.05 it means that  only 5 it is only five percent likely to  draw a sample  with a difference of 250 euro or more  if there's no difference between the  salary and the population  so if there's no difference  in the population it is only four  percent  likely to draw a sample in which  the difference in the salary is more  than 250 euro  so if the p-value is quite low for sure  we can ask if the null hypothesis is  true  in the population maybe the difference  in the salary difference is not  zero in the population so and now we  come to the question  at which point is it unlikely enough to  reject  the null hypothesis therefore we come to  the question  at what point is the null hypothesis  rejected  for this purpose the significance level  also called alpha level  is introduced it is very important that  the significance level is always  determined before the examination and  must not be  changed afterward in order to still  obtain  the desired statement  to ensure a certain degree of  comparability the significance level is  usually  five percent or one percent  the alpha level below one percent is  highly significant  below five percent is significant and  above five percent it is not significant  so and now we will come again to the  p-value  the definition of the p-value is that's  a probability  of the observed result plus even more  extreme  results if the null hypothesis is true  and let's have a look at our example  again what  does this mean if the null hypothesis is  true  is that we assume in the population  there's no  difference between the salary of woman  and man so we ask  what is the probability that we draw a  sample  where the salary differ by more than 